🟢 Page vs Slice (Important Interview Point)
1️⃣ Page
Page<User> page = repo.findAll(pageable);

It:

Executes main query

Executes an extra COUNT(*) query

So it gives:

totalElements

totalPages

hasNext()

etc.

⚠️ Slightly slower because of count query.



2️⃣ Slice
Slice<User> slice = repo.findAll(pageable);

It:

Does NOT run count query

Only checks if next page exists

⚡ Faster than Page

Used in:

Infinite scroll

Large datasets



🚨 Important: Does Spring Support Cursor Pagination?

❌ Not automatically.

Spring Data JPA does NOT provide built-in cursor pagination.

If you want cursor pagination, you must write custom query like:

@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.id > :lastId ORDER BY u.id ASC")
List<User> findNextUsers(@Param("lastId") Long lastId, Pageable pageable);

That is manual keyset pagination.



🔥 Interview-Level Answer

If interviewer asks:

What pagination strategy does Spring Boot use?

You say:

By default, Spring Data JPA uses offset-based pagination with LIMIT and OFFSET. The Page object also triggers a count query.
 For better performance in large datasets, we can use Slice or implement custom cursor-based pagination.